WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.510 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  it's summer 2018.  i'm sitting at my desk and one of my  clients walks in  he drops his bag on the floor and turns  to the others in the office asking what  they think of the latest headline about  brett kavanaugh's nomination to the  supreme court  as the 10 men around me casually debate  whether he had actually assaulted dr  blasey ford  i feel my chest heightened  i see them speaking but their voices  drop off and i can't hear anything  i stumble to the bathroom in search of  privacy  as the memories begin to flood my brain  i bury my face my palms i can't stop  them  suddenly someone walks in the bathroom  and the noise is enough to jolt me back  into the present  i take a deep shaky breath and walk back  to my desk as if nothing happened  these episodes continued and became  unbearable  i knew i needed help and finally made an  appointment with a therapist who would  change my life  she helped me see that these were ptsd  like flashbacks common among trauma  survivors  i had thought these were work-related  anxiety attacks  i had no idea and i hadn't even realized  that my client's comments were  triggering flashbacks to my own  experience with sexual assault an  experience i'd suppressed for years  now rearing its head  it was clear i would need to make time  for consistent regular therapy to  address these flashbacks and their  underlying cause  unfortunately the only time available in  my therapist practice was tuesday  mornings at 9 30 a.m  right in the middle of the weekly update  to our clients that i usually led  i dreaded bringing this up with my boss  i didn't want him to think i was weak by  disclosing that i was dealing with a  mental health issue  i decided to frame it vaguely around a  medical issue  surely he'd understand that  i approached him one day on our way out  of work  stumbling over my words as i tried to  blurt them out as quickly as possible  i need to see a doctor weekly for  treatment and the only time available is  tuesdays at 9 30 am  i know that conflicts with our weekly  client meetings but this is a priority  for me and i'm hoping you can help me  he looked concerned  is this a serious medical issue would  extended medical leave be a better  option  oh no  now he actually thought i had a serious  medical condition  i decided to backtrack and tell him the  truth  no no it's nothing serious well  it is serious  i'm going through a tough time and i  need to see a therapist weekly  he nodded slowly  are there any other days of the week you  could see your therapist  could you see them before or after  working hours  i shook my head no this is the only time  available  maybe you could go every other week  no i need to see this person every week  that's non-negotiable  looked at me for a beat and i saw  something shift in his face  you know what  do what you have to do we'll make it  work  as i reflect back on this experience it  makes me sad to realize that the only  way i thought my mental health would be  taken seriously was to claim a physical  illness  if in 2018 i'd torn my acl and needed  physical therapy i would have had no  problem going to my boss  so why did it feel so much harder to  share my mental health needs  one year later i myself was leading a  team on a high profile high stress  project with one of our largest clients  and i could tell something was wrong  one of the associates on my team we'll  call her rachel usually very focused and  high performing seemed distracted  i checked in with her one-on-one and she  shared that she was struggling with  depression but unable to consistently  see her therapist because of our travel  schedules  as consultants we typically traveled to  be on-site at our client monday to  thursday spending three nights on the  road  my heart ached for rachel when she told  me this  i thought back to my moment of crisis  year earlier and i wanted to help her  get the support she needed  but again i was nervous to bring it up  with my boss  i was concerned he might think that i  and the team couldn't handle the  pressures of the job which we'd signed  up for knowing involved travel  and i was up for a promotion and i was  worried my manager might doubt my  leadership abilities if i didn't tell  rachel no we have to be there this is  what the client wants  but i wasn't willing to tell her no i  wanted to help her get the support she  needed i knew firsthand the impact that  an hour a week with my therapist had  made on my mental health and i wanted  the same for her  i set up time with my boss and i told  him the team wanted to reduce our number  of days on the road  he initially pushed back saying the  clients aren't paying us to sit at home  but eventually after i emphasized the  physical and mental toll this was taking  on the team he agreed to try out a  three-day week travel schedule  the team stuck with it and rachel was  able to see her therapist every thursday  now  i don't claim to be the model of  effective management after all this was  before my mba  but the impact that my personal  experience had had on me allowed me to  empathize with rachel and help her get  the support she needs  i recognize not every work environment  will be this flexible but in the white  collar salaried work environments many  of us will go into after we graduate  why is it so hard to imagine a work  environment where employees can  prioritize their mental health even if  it's just an hour a week for therapy  my view is that making space for mental  health at work is hard because the  stigma around mental health still exists  80 percent of employees reported stigma  at the in the workplace about mental  health in a recent survey from april  2021  which is astounding when you realize  that in reality 20 of american adults  struggle with mental illness  let that sink in  one in five american adults you walk by  on the street see in the grocery store  and who are even sitting in this room  are struggling with their mental health  on a daily basis  and to make matters worse less than half  of those people get the treatment or  support they need  to put it into context one in five  americans has been diagnosed with  covid19 to date the same prevalence as  mental illness  mental health is the pandemic we're not  talking about  our deep prioritization of mental health  isn't a new challenge  however  we have structurally valued mental  health to a lesser degree than physical  health  mental health benefits are less likely  to be covered by health insurance plans  and are reimbursed at lower rates when  they are  the number of federally funded residency  positions in psychiatry has remained  flat since the 1990s even as the  population has grown  and demand for mental health services  has soared  these are just a few of the systemic  barriers preventing access to mental  affordable mental health services  today i want to focus on what we all can  do in our everyday lives to break down  the stigma around mental health and help  people take that first step towards  seeking care  i believe we can accomplish this by  shifting our perspective around mental  health to be equally as important as our  physical health  prevention trauma and recovery exist in  both realms  why should we think differently of  either  we can start by normalizing mental  health at home in our private lives and  in conversations with friends and family  and also in our public lives at work  it's at work where i see the greatest  potential for a shift in stigma around  mental health  now much has been written about what  corporations can do at an organizational  level to support employee mental health  i want to focus on what we all can do in  our everyday interactions at work to  support those struggling with mental  health  we can start by checking in on our  people and asking those on our teams  what they need to be at their best  hear your employees and respect their  needs without judging whether their  needs are worth investing time into  therapy  ask yourself if an employee or colleague  of yours needed physical therapy would  you respond differently  and don't underestimate your role in  creating a supportive work environment  through small changes to your daily  schedule  i challenge you to ask these questions  of yourselves to better support those  struggling with mental health at work  can you be the one to role model what it  looks like to make space for mental  health at work  it might make a world of a difference to  someone on your team  as it did for me  i still see a psychologist  every week four years later  and i am still working through the  effects of the trauma i experienced  but the impact of managers who respected  the boundaries i said at work cannot be  overstated  they took my mental health seriously and  respected my needs when i made them  known  my team was fine without being at that  weekly client meeting  all i needed was a compassionate manager  who was willing to step in for me  i challenge you to be that compassionate  manager for those on your teams and in  your workplace  thank you  [Music]

